  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 235 AM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\n*Calm Conditions Persist Through Tonight*\n\nA 1017 mb surface high is currently meandering eastward across\nnorthern/central Illinois, promoting clear skies, light E/NE \nwinds, and comfortable conditions. Efficient radiational cooling\nis allowing temperatures to cool into the low 60s (some spots \ncould locally dip into the upper 50s by daybreak). At the same \ntime, GOES-19 nighttime microphysics imagery reveals some patchy\nshallow fog has been developing in Vermilion and Edgar \ncounties, though is transient in nature and poses minimal risk \nfor notable visibility reductions. Any rural fog will quickly \nburn off as the sun rises this morning.\n\nAs the anticyclone drifts into Indiana today, winds will veer to\nbecome southeasterly by midday. Dew points near 60 degrees and\nafternoon highs in the mid 80s will keep conditions \ncomfortable. As the next weather system begins to approach, thin\ncirrus clouds will begin to spill into central Illinois this \nmorning, gradually thickening into the afternoon, filtering out \nmore sunshine.\n\n*Heavy Rain and Localized Flooding on Friday and Saturday*\n\nA mid-level shortwave traversing across Montana and the Dakotas\natop the ridge today will become further enhanced by \nconvective/MCS activity in the northern Plains before digging \nsoutheastward through the Midwest. This system is a bit unusual \nfor late July, with a sfc low pinwheeling its way through the \narea Friday and Saturday, accompanied by a wave of showers and \nthunderstorms. The chance of thunderstorms is highest Friday \nnight as elevated instability (MUCAPE of 1000-1500 J/kg) \noverspreads central and southeastern Illinois. SPC SWODY2 keeps \nthe risk for severe weather just to our southwest, as the main \ninstability axis will reside in Missouri Friday afternoon. \nHowever, will have to monitor the speed of the sfc low, as a \nquicker progression could push this risk more into western \nIllinois. On Saturday, lingering showers and storms underneath \nthe low may have the tendency to produce a few cold air funnels\n(mainly across SE IL), given the vorticity-rich environment in \nplace.\n\nThe precipitation associated with this system could result in\nheavy rainfall and flooding in some areas, given PWATs in the\n1.9-2.2 inch range and deep column moisture. WPC Day 2 (Friday)\nERO has a slight (15-39%) risk of excessive rainfall across \nmuch of the area, mainly west of I-57. On Saturday, WPC \nmaintains a marginal (5-15%) risk of flooding east of the \nI-55/I-155 corridor. Slow storm motions and training of \nconvective cells is the main concern. Broader precipitation \nshield along the northern side of the low will promote steady \nlight-moderate rain rates. There remains some disagreement among\nguidance on the exact track of this system (GFS/NAM weaker & \nfurther south, ECMWF stronger & further north). In the scenario \nof a stronger low, a more well-defined mid-level dry slot could \nslightly reduce rainfall totals across central IL, keeping the \nbulk mainly north, though confidence in this is low. The initial\npush of showers is expected Friday AM from west to east and \nshould conclude sometime late Saturday. While there will be some\nlingering low-level dry air across central IL ahead of this, \ntop-down saturation should occur rather quickly. The global \nmodels suggest the potential for a deformation zone along the NW\nside of the system, which would keep rainfall going into the \nevening, so this will have to be monitored.\n\n*Seasonable Temperatures Return by Next Week*\n\nWidespread clouds and precipitation on Friday and Saturday will\nput a lid on temperatures, with afternoon highs as low as the\nmid 70s both days. On Friday, our southeastern counties should\nsee the rain hold off until the early afternoon, allowing a few\nextra hours for temperatures to climb into the low-mid 80s. Some\nclouds may linger on Sunday, but surface high pressure will\nbegin to build back in and clear skies out through Tuesday. Each\nday, high temperatures will climb an additional 1-2 degrees (to\nthe mid 80s), with dew points in the mid-upper 60s.\n\nBy Wednesday, a piece of a cutoff mid-upper low over TN/KY may \ndrift back up into the area as it joins the jet stream, \nbringing a low (20-30%) chance of showers across our southeast.\nOtherwise, a cold front hung up in the Midwest should remain \nnorthwest of the ILX CWA, though may begin nosing into the area \nmid-week.",
